\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBlibusb_get_device_string()\fP 

.PP
\fBlibusb_get_interface_string()\fP
.RE
.PP
Like \fBlibusb_get_device_string()\fP this function works while the device is still closed, relying on the operating system's cached descriptors\&. Unlike \fBlibusb_get_device_string()\fP the result is not cached by libusb, so each call may perform I/O; cache the string if it is needed repeatedly\&.

.PP
Platform notes:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Windows and macOS fetch the string by its descriptor index and can serve any configuration, active or not\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Linux reads the string from sysfs, which only exposes the string of the active configuration\&. A non-active configuration can only be served when it shares its string descriptor (iConfiguration index) with the active one; otherwise \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\fP is returned even though the requested configuration is valid\&. In that case the string can still be read by opening the device and calling \fBlibusb_get_string_descriptor()\fP with the iConfiguration index\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Backends that do not implement this function always return \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\fP\&. 
.PP

.SS "int libusb_get_interface_string (\fBlibusb_device\fP * dev, uint8_t config_value, uint8_t interface_number, uint8_t alt_setting, char * data, int length)"

.PP
Retrieve the string descriptor describing an interface (iInterface) without needing to open the device\&. Since version 1\&.0\&.31, \fBLIBUSB_API_VERSION\fP >= 0x0100010D

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdev\fP the target device 
.br
\fIconfig_value\fP the bConfigurationValue of the configuration the interface belongs to, or 0 to select the device's currently active configuration\&. 
.br
\fIinterface_number\fP the bInterfaceNumber of the interface 
.br
\fIalt_setting\fP the bAlternateSetting of the interface 
.br
\fIdata\fP the data buffer for the UTF-8 encoded string, or NULL to only determine the required buffer size (length must still be non-zero)\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP the size of the data buffer in bytes\&. USB string descriptors cannot be longer than LIBUSB_DEVICE_STRING_BYTES_MAX\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
a negative error code or the actual string length in bytes including the null terminator\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBlibusb_get_device_string()\fP 

.PP
\fBlibusb_get_config_string()\fP
.RE
.PP
Like \fBlibusb_get_device_string()\fP this function works while the device is still closed, relying on the operating system's cached descriptors\&. Unlike \fBlibusb_get_device_string()\fP the result is not cached by libusb, so each call may perform I/O; cache the string if it is needed repeatedly\&.

.PP
A return of \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_FOUND\fP means the interface has no string descriptor (its iInterface is 0) or the requested interface/alternate setting does not exist\&.

.PP
Platform notes:
.IP "\(bu" 2
Windows and macOS fetch the string by its descriptor index and can serve any configuration and alternate setting, current or not\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Linux reads the string from sysfs, which only exposes the string of the currently selected alternate setting in the active configuration\&. A valid but non-current alternate setting (or non-active configuration) can only be served when it shares its string descriptor (iInterface index) with the current one; otherwise \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\fP is returned even though the requested combination is valid\&. In that case the string can still be read by opening the device and calling \fBlibusb_get_string_descriptor()\fP with the iInterface index\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Backends that do not implement this function always return \fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\fP\&. 
.PP

.SS "int libusb_get_descriptor (\fBlibusb_device_handle\fP * dev_handle, uint8_t desc_type, uint8_t desc_index, unsigned char * data, int length)\fR [inline]\fP, \fR [static]\fP"

.PP
Retrieve a descriptor from the default control pipe\&. This is a convenience function which formulates the appropriate control message to retrieve the descriptor\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdev_handle\fP a device handle 
.br
\fIdesc_type\fP the descriptor type, see \fBlibusb_descriptor_type\fP 
.br
\fIdesc_index\fP the index of the descriptor to retrieve 
.br
\fIdata\fP output buffer for descriptor 
.br
\fIlength\fP size of data buffer 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
number of bytes returned in data, or LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_get_string_descriptor (\fBlibusb_device_handle\fP * dev_handle, uint8_t desc_index, uint16_t langid, unsigned char * data, int length)\fR [inline]\fP, \fR [static]\fP"

.PP
Retrieve a descriptor from a device\&. This is a convenience function which formulates the appropriate control message to retrieve the descriptor\&. The string returned is Unicode, as detailed in the USB specifications\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdev_handle\fP a device handle 
.br
\fIdesc_index\fP the index of the descriptor to retrieve 
.br
\fIlangid\fP the language ID for the string descriptor 
.br
\fIdata\fP output buffer for descriptor 
.br
\fIlength\fP size of data buffer 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
number of bytes returned in data, or LIBUSB_ERROR code on failure 
.RE
